    tc: "O(1) get and put",
    sc: "O(capacity)",
    sig: "<b>Signal:</b> 'design a cache', 'evict the least recently used', or any API where <em>both</em> lookup and reordering must be O(1). The giveaway is a capacity plus a recency rule.",
    why: "A hash map gives O(1) lookup but no order. A doubly linked list gives O(1) removal <em>from the middle</em> but no lookup. Combine them: the map stores key → node, the list stores recency. Every operation is a map lookup plus a constant number of pointer rewires.",
    trap: "<b>A singly linked list cannot do this.</b> To unlink a node you need its predecessor, and finding that is O(n) — which quietly turns your O(1) cache into O(n) and is the exact thing the question is testing. Also: <code>get</code> must promote the entry, not just read it. A <code>get</code> that does not reorder is the single most common wrong answer.",
    say: "Hash map for lookup, doubly linked list for recency, sentinel head and tail so no branch ever tests for null. Both operations are O(1).",
    py: "class Node:\n    __slots__ = ('k', 'v', 'prev', 'next')\n    def __init__(self, k=0, v=0):\n        self.k, self.v = k, v\n        self.prev = self.next = None\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, cap):\n        self.cap = cap\n        self.m = {}\n        self.head, self.tail = Node(), Node()   # sentinels: no null checks\n        self.head.next, self.tail.prev = self.tail, self.head\n\n    def _unlink(self, n):\n        n.prev.next, n.next.prev = n.next, n.prev\n\n    def _push_front(self, n):\n        n.next, n.prev = self.head.next, self.head\n        self.head.next.prev = n\n        self.head.next = n\n\n    def get(self, k):\n        if k not in self.m:\n            return -1\n        n = self.m[k]\n        self._unlink(n); self._push_front(n)     # PROMOTE on read\n        return n.v\n\n    def put(self, k, v):\n        if k in self.m:\n            n = self.m[k]; n.v = v\n            self._unlink(n); self._push_front(n)\n            return\n        if len(self.m) == self.cap:\n            lru = self.tail.prev\n            self._unlink(lru); del self.m[lru.k]  # delete by KEY, not node\n        n = Node(k, v)\n        self.m[k] = n; self._push_front(n)\n\n# Interview shortcut, if allowed:\n# from collections import OrderedDict -> move_to_end / popitem(last=False)",
    cpp: "class LRUCache {\n    int cap;\n    list<pair<int,int>> dll;                   // front = most recent\n    unordered_map<int, list<pair<int,int>>::iterator> m;\npublic:\n    LRUCache(int c) : cap(c) {}\n\n    int get(int k) {\n        auto it = m.find(k);\n        if (it == m.end()) return -1;\n        dll.splice(dll.begin(), dll, it->second);  // O(1) promote\n        return it->second->second;\n    }\n\n    void put(int k, int v) {\n        auto it = m.find(k);\n        if (it != m.end()) {\n            it->second->second = v;\n            dll.splice(dll.begin(), dll, it->second);\n            return;\n        }\n        if ((int) m.size() == cap) {\n            m.erase(dll.back().first);            // erase by key\n            dll.pop_back();\n        }\n        dll.push_front({k, v});\n        m[k] = dll.begin();\n    }\n};",
    java: "class LRUCache {\n    private final int cap;\n    private final LinkedHashMap<Integer,Integer> m;\n\n    public LRUCache(int cap) {\n        this.cap = cap;\n        // accessOrder = true makes get() reorder\n        this.m = new LinkedHashMap<>(16, 0.75f, true) {\n            protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<Integer,Integer> e) {\n                return size() > LRUCache.this.cap;\n            }\n        };\n    }\n    public int get(int k)          { return m.getOrDefault(k, -1); }\n    public void put(int k, int v)  { m.put(k, v); }\n}\n// Say out loud that you know the manual hash + doubly-linked-list version;\n// some interviewers ban LinkedHashMap precisely because it hides the point.",
