    say: "Quickselect averages O(n) because I only recurse into the side containing k, and I randomise the pivot so sorted input isn't the quadratic case.",
    py: "import random\n\ndef quickselect(a, k):\n    \"\"\"k is 0-indexed into the sorted order\"\"\"\n    lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1\n    while lo <= hi:\n        p = random.randint(lo, hi)          # randomise: kills the sorted worst case\n        a[p], a[hi] = a[hi], a[p]\n        pivot, store = a[hi], lo\n        for i in range(lo, hi):\n            if a[i] < pivot:\n                a[i], a[store] = a[store], a[i]\n                store += 1\n        a[store], a[hi] = a[hi], a[store]   # pivot to final place\n        if store == k:\n            return a[store]\n        if store < k:\n            lo = store + 1                  # recurse ONE side only\n        else:\n            hi = store - 1\n    return -1\n\n# kth LARGEST = quickselect(a, len(a) - k)",
